#[chapter/05/lore]{
  notes: [
    "Two claims on the wish — a boon-claim and a bane-claim — cannot both post. The rift is the song of that disagreement made audible."
    "Wish-physics, taught properly: a wish redirects momentum the world already has. Small and aligned costs one coincidence and a Thursday; grand and crosswise bills a correction to someone e..."
    "The rift itself is old correction-debt from a grandiose wish granted centuries ago and never paid down. The land pays the interest a hairline at a time."
    "The egg warms the crack and leaves it honest. A wish does not reconcile other people's wanting."
    "Hum-crickets live in the Quiet Rift's crack and keep its pitch. They go silent when someone lies near the floor — in either sense; nobody has ever told them which."
    "The crack-gauge, brass calipers set across the rift, measures in notes, not inches; each morning a clerk takes the note. With the egg in the hall it rang a note nobody had written yet — t..."
    "A carpenter's door-rule lies folded on the hall's sill, shut on a size that fits no door in the room. Nobody has claimed it."
    "Listen before you mend: the clerks' rule for the singing floor. The crack's note is taken and written in the margin before anyone touches the slate. A floor once leaded over unheard sang ..."
  ]
}

